// Reaching a register at all — the one thing every other module here rests on.

module sh.sysl.rp2040blocks.mmio
@no_alloc

import sysl.raw.ptr_cast

// A register, as a place the compiler may not reason about.
//
// **The `volatile` is the whole of what this function adds over arithmetic.** An optimizer is
// entitled to assume that reading the same storage twice yields the same value, that a store nobody
// reads is a store nobody needs, and that two accesses in a row may be one wider access. All three
// are false at a device: `while *reg(STATUS) & BUSY != 0 do ()` is a loop that reads until the
// hardware changes it, and a compiler that hoisted the read out would spin forever on the first
// value it saw.
//
// `ptr_cast` gets you the address. This gets you a *correct* driver.
reg(addr: u32) -> *volatile u32 = ptr_cast(usize(addr))

// ## The atomic aliases
//
// **Every peripheral register on this chip has three more addresses above it**, at fixed offsets
// from the block's base, and writing to one of them is a read-modify-write the hardware does
// indivisibly:
//
// | alias | offset | writing a mask |
// |---|---|---|
// | XOR | `0x1000` | flips those bits |
// | SET | `0x2000` | raises those bits |
// | CLR | `0x3000` | lowers those bits |
//
// **This is not a convenience.** A read-modify-write done in software races the other core, and the
// registers most worth changing one bit of — `RESETS`, the pad controls, the GPIO output — are
// exactly the ones both cores reach. It also costs one store rather than three instructions, which
// is why it is worth reaching for even in code that will never run on the second core.
//
// The offsets go on the *register's* address, not the block's: `mmio.set(resets.BASE +
// resets.RESET_OFFSET)` is the reset register's set alias.
const XOR: u32 = 0x1000
const SET: u32 = 0x2000
const CLR: u32 = 0x3000

// The three aliases of a register whose ordinary address is `addr`.
xor(addr: u32) -> *volatile u32 = reg(addr + XOR)
set(addr: u32) -> *volatile u32 = reg(addr + SET)
clr(addr: u32) -> *volatile u32 = reg(addr + CLR)
